  "verbalization": "Cyclacillin and Slc22a5 share a common pathway through the beta-lactam antibiotic class, as evidenced by their structural resemblance to Azlocillin and Mezlocillin, both of which bind to Slc22a5. The lowest common ancestors of Cyclacillin and its relatives—Penicillin and Beta-Lactam Antibiotics—establish a hierarchical relationship that links the drug to the target protein. This suggests that Cyclacillin, through its structural similarity to other beta-lactam antibiotics, may exert a functional effect similar to those of its relatives, which in turn bind to Slc22a5. The evidence supports a mechanism where the structural and functional properties of beta-lactam antibiotics, through their binding to Slc22a5, are preserved or transmitted via evolutionary or structural homology. The integration of these paths highlights a potential dependency on beta-lactam antibiotics, which may explain the observed interaction between Cyclacillin and Slc22a5.",
